wi6vFmVaHM5BqzyGFeSNwVP9jbCfwOPQLeIxanAewXvb54K5J5CGRhrdXLj_4Ve0Z4wCq4h1abVfpln1vgPVkJAzr3xQ/D_r4-8R4y_ufDjQsNHM_wA_YRq1mwlha/22>\n |SIGN UP <https://www.washingtonpost.com/newsletters/?> \n <https://www.washingtonpost.com/> \n\n\n\n \n<https://sli.washingtonpost.com/click?s=1069859&e=luisdomingogarcia79@gmail.com&p=19019527&lctg=3956a6a8-edfc-467f-b5e4-47dc9c0d30da&stpe=default>\n \n<https://sli.washingtonpost.com/click?s=1069859&e=luisdomingogarcia79@gmail.com&p=19019527&lctg=3956a6a8-edfc-467f-b5e4-47dc9c0d30da&stpe=default>\n\n\n\n \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/style/interactive/2026/07/15/25-most-influential-works-american-culture/>\n\n (Getty/The Washington Post) \n\n\nThe 25 most influential works of American culture \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/style/interactive/2026/07/15/25-most-influential-works-american-culture/>\n\nAre we a young country or an old one? By the standards of ancient Egypt, or \nChina, we have been on the map for the smallest blip of time. But what an \nastonishing interval that has been. Since the founders put their names to the \nDeclaration of Independence, there have been revolutions in science, \ntechnology, medicine, art and literature. We have seen the camera transform \nmemory and politics, we have doubled our average lifespan, and we have been to \nthe moon.\n\nThere’s no summing up the United States, perhaps because we still don’t know \nwho we are and what we want to be when we grow up. We didn’t will ourselves \ninto a coherent nation but kept uncovering new things, discovering new voices, \nalways expanding, unearthing, unbinding. No decade can be summed up any more \nthan a country can be summed up, but if we wrote an autobiography not of words \nbut of works — books, music and art, ideas, dress and culture — these 25 would \nbe among the most momentous. These are not necessarily our proudest moments, \nbut they are defining acts of culture. It is an imperfect list, and incomplete, \nbut then that is America, imperfect and unfinished. So we hope.\n\n\nRead more \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/style/interactive/2026/07/15/25-most-influential-works-american-culture/>\n\n\n \n<https://sli.washingtonpost.com/click?s=1017604&e=luisdomingogarcia79@gmail.com&p=19019527&lctg=3956a6a8-edfc-467f-b5e4-47dc9c0d30da&stpe=default>\n \n<https://sli.washingtonpost.com/click?s=1017604&e=luisdomingogarcia79@gmail.com&p=19019527&lctg=3956a6a8-edfc-467f-b5e4-47dc9c0d30da&stpe=default>\n\n\nMore Must Reads\n\n\nWhy recruiters can’t find workers and new grads can’t find jobs (it’s not AI) \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/education/2026/07/12/why-recruiters-cant-find-workers-new-grads-cant-find-jobs/>\n \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/education/2026/07/12/why-recruiters-cant-find-workers-new-grads-cant-find-jobs/>\n (Scott Olson/Getty)\n\nExperts say a major labor shortage looms because of population shifts and a \nmismatch between new graduates’ skills and employers’ needs. \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/education/2026/07/12/why-recruiters-cant-find-workers-new-grads-cant-find-jobs/>\n\nBy Jon Marcus\n\n\nRead more \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/education/2026/07/12/why-recruiters-cant-find-workers-new-grads-cant-find-jobs/>\n <https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?text=Why recruiters can’t find workers and \nnew grads can’t find jobs (it’s not \nAI)&url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.washingtonpost.com%2Feducation%2F2026%2F07%2F12%2Fwhy-recruiters-cant-find-workers-new-grads-cant-find-jobs%2F%3Futm_campaign%3Dwp_must_reads%26utm_medium%3Dsocial_share%26utm_source%3Dtwitter%26wpisrc%3Dnl_must-reads>\n \n<https://www.facebook.com/sharer/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.washingtonpost.com%2Feducation%2F2026%2F07%2F12%2Fwhy-recruiters-cant-find-workers-new-grads-cant-find-jobs%2F?utm_campaign=wp_must_reads&utm_medium=social_share&utm_source=facebook&wpisrc=nl_must-reads>\n <mailto:?subject=Check out this article from Must Reads newsletter&body=Hey! I \nthink you'd really like this article. Here is the link: \nhttps%3A%2F%2Fwww.washingtonpost.com%2Feducation%2F2026%2F07%2F12%2Fwhy-recruiters-cant-find-workers-new-grads-cant-find-jobs%2F%3Futm_campaign%3Dwp_must_reads%26utm_medium%3Dsocial_share%26utm_source%3Dnewsletter%26wpisrc%3Dnl_must-reads>\n\nGuest Column  Monica Romano \nI smoked cannabis for nearly 20 years. Here’s what I wish I knew at 13. \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/wellness/2026/07/14/i-smoked-cannabis-nearly-20-years-heres-what-i-wish-i-knew-13/>\n \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/wellness/2026/07/14/i-smoked-cannabis-nearly-20-years-heres-what-i-wish-i-knew-13/>\n (iStock)\n\nAs cannabis use becomes more widespread, we should be clear-eyed about some of \nthe potential long-term effects. \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/wellness/2026/07/14/i-smoked-cannabis-nearly-20-years-heres-what-i-wish-i-knew-13/>\n\nBy Monica Romano\n\n\nRead more \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/wellness/2026/07/14/i-smoked-cannabis-nearly-20-years-heres-what-i-wish-i-knew-13/>\n <https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?text=I smoked cannabis for nearly 20 years. \nHere’s what I wish I knew at \n13.&url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.washingtonpost.com%2Fwellness%2F2026%2F07%2F14%2Fi-smoked-cannabis-nearly-20-years-heres-what-i-wish-i-knew-13%2F%3Futm_campaign%3Dwp_must_reads%26utm_medium%3Dsocial_share%26utm_source%3Dtwitter%26wpisrc%3Dnl_must-reads>\n \n<https://www.facebook.com/sharer/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.washingtonpost.com%2Fwellness%2F2026%2F07%2F14%2Fi-smoked-cannabis-nearly-20-years-heres-what-i-wish-i-knew-13%2F?utm_campaign=wp_must_reads&utm_medium=social_share&utm_source=facebook&wpisrc=nl_must-reads>\n <mailto:?subject=Check out this article from Must Reads newsletter&body=Hey! I \nthink you'd really like this article. Here is the link: \nhttps%3A%2F%2Fwww.washingtonpost.com%2Fwellness%2F2026%2F07%2F14%2Fi-smoked-cannabis-nearly-20-years-heres-what-i-wish-i-knew-13%2F%3Futm_campaign%3Dwp_must_reads%26utm_medium%3Dsocial_share%26utm_source%3Dnewsletter%26wpisrc%3Dnl_must-reads>\n\n\nReflecting Pool peeling probably caused by application flaws, experts say \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/investigations/interactive/2026/07/16/reflecting-pool-peeling-likely-caused-by-application-flaws-experts-say/>\n \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/investigations/interactive/2026/07/16/reflecting-pool-peeling-likely-caused-by-application-flaws-experts-say/>\n (The Washington Post)\n\nPresident Donald Trump has blamed vandals, but a Washington Post analysis \nfinds that the peeling tracks closely with stages of the renovation process. \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/investigations/interactive/2026/07/16/reflecting-pool-peeling-likely-caused-by-application-flaws-experts-say/>\n\nBy Washington Post Staff\n\n\nRead more \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/investigations/interactive/2026/07/16/reflecting-pool-peeling-likely-caused-by-application-flaws-experts-say/>\n <https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?text=Reflecting Pool peeling probably caused \nby application flaws, experts \nsay&url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.washingtonpost.com%2Finvestigations%2Finteractive%2F2026%2F07%2F16%2Freflecting-pool-peeling-likely-caused-by-application-flaws-experts-say%2F%3Futm_campaign%3Dwp_must_reads%26utm_medium%3Dsocial_share%26utm_source%3Dtwitter%26wpisrc%3Dnl_must-reads>\n \n<https://www.facebook.com/sharer/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.washingtonpost.com%2Finvestigations%2Finteractive%2F2026%2F07%2F16%2Freflecting-pool-peeling-likely-caused-by-application-flaws-experts-say%2F?utm_campaign=wp_must_reads&utm_medium=social_share&utm_source=facebook&wpisrc=nl_must-reads>\n <mailto:?subject=Check out this article from Must Reads newsletter&body=Hey! I \nthink you'd really like this article. Here is the link: \nhttps%3A%2F%2Fwww.washingtonpost.com%2Finvestigations%2Finteractive%2F2026%2F07%2F16%2Freflecting-pool-peeling-likely-caused-by-application-flaws-experts-say%2F%3Futm_campaign%3Dwp_must_reads%26utm_medium%3Dsocial_share%26utm_source%3Dnewsletter%26wpisrc%3Dnl_must-reads>\n\n\nSurvivors of Iranian attack that killed 6 U.S. troops say generals ignored \nwarnings \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/national-security/2026/07/12/survivors-iranian-attack-that-killed-6-us-troops-say-generals-ignored-warnings/>\n \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/national-security/2026/07/12/survivors-iranian-attack-that-killed-6-us-troops-say-generals-ignored-warnings/>\n (Anna Moneymaker/Getty)\n\nThe drone strike on Port Shuaiba in Kuwait occurred on the second day of the \nwar. Those who were there say they fear no one will be held accountable. \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/national-security/2026/07/12/survivors-iranian-attack-that-killed-6-us-troops-say-generals-ignored-warnings/>\n\nBy Tara Copp and Alex Horton\n\n\nRead more \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/national-security/2026/07/12/survivors-iranian-attack-that-killed-6-us-troops-say-generals-ignored-warnings/>\n <https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?text=Survivors of Iranian attack that killed \n6 U.S. troops say generals ignored \nwarnings&url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.washingtonpost.com%2Fnational-security%2F2026%2F07%2F12%2Fsurvivors-iranian-attack-that-killed-6-us-troops-say-generals-ignored-warnings%2F%3Futm_campaign%3Dwp_must_reads%26utm_medium%3Dsocial_share%26utm_source%3Dtwitter%26wpisrc%3Dnl_must-reads>\n \n<https://www.facebook.com/sharer/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.washingtonpost.com%2Fnational-security%2F2026%2F07%2F12%2Fsurvivors-iranian-attack-that-killed-6-us-troops-say-generals-ignored-warnings%2F?utm_campaign=wp_must_reads&utm_medium=social_share&utm_source=facebook&wpisrc=nl_must-reads>\n <mailto:?subject=Check out this article from Must Reads newsletter&body=Hey! I \nthink you'd really like this article. Here is the link: \nhttps%3A%2F%2Fwww.washingtonpost.com%2Fnational-security%2F2026%2F07%2F12%2Fsurvivors-iranian-attack-that-killed-6-us-troops-say-generals-ignored-warnings%2F%3Futm_campaign%3Dwp_must_reads%26utm_medium%3Dsocial_share%26utm_source%3Dnewsletter%26wpisrc%3Dnl_must-reads>\n\n\n\n\n \n<https://sli.washingtonpost.com/click?s=176704&e=luisdomingogarcia79@gmail.com&p=19019527&lctg=3956a6a8-edfc-467f-b5e4-47dc9c0d30da&stpe=default>\n \n<https://sli.washingtonpost.com/click?s=176704&e=luisdomingogarcia79@gmail.com&p=19019527&lctg=3956a6a8-edfc-467f-b5e4-47dc9c0d30da&stpe=default>\n\nColumn  Monica Hesse \nEvery era gets the ‘Odyssey’ it deserves \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/style/2026/07/17/what-even-is-the-odyssey/> \n <https://www.washingtonpost.com/style/2026/07/17/what-even-is-the-odyssey/> \n (Melinda Sue Gordon/Universal Pictures via AP)\n\nHere’s how to prepare your brain for Christopher Nolan’s new adaptation. \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/style/2026/07/17/what-even-is-the-odyssey/> \n\n\nRead more \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/style/2026/07/17/what-even-is-the-odyssey/>  \n<https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?text=Every era gets the ‘Odyssey’ it deserves \n&url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.washingtonpost.com%2Fstyle%2F2026%2F07%2F17%2Fwhat-even-is-the-odyssey%2F%3Futm_campaign%3Dwp_must_reads%26utm_medium%3Dsocial_share%26utm_source%3Dtwitter%26wpisrc%3Dnl_must-reads>\n \n<https://www.facebook.com/sharer/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.washingtonpost.com%2Fstyle%2F2026%2F07%2F17%2Fwhat-even-is-the-odyssey%2F?utm_campaign=wp_must_reads&utm_medium=social_share&utm_source=facebook&wpisrc=nl_must-reads>\n <mailto:?subject=Check out this article from Must Reads newsletter&body=Hey! I \nthink you'd really like this article. Here is the link: \nhttps%3A%2F%2Fwww.washingtonpost.com%2Fstyle%2F2026%2F07%2F17%2Fwhat-even-is-the-odyssey%2F%3Futm_campaign%3Dwp_must_reads%26utm_medium%3Dsocial_share%26utm_source%3Dnewsletter%26wpisrc%3Dnl_must-reads>\n\n\nA duck and a dog formed an unlikely friendship. Now they’re inseparable. \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/lifestyle/2026/07/12/duck-dog-formed-an-unlikely-friendship-now-theyre-inseparable/>\n \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/lifestyle/2026/07/12/duck-dog-formed-an-unlikely-friendship-now-theyre-inseparable/>\n (Tori Cannarelli)\n\nEpilepsy medication helped the golden retriever, but it dulled her \npersonality. The arrival of a 4-day-old duckling sparked new life in Barley. \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/lifestyle/2026/07/12/duck-dog-formed-an-unlikely-friendship-now-theyre-inseparable/>\n\nBy Sydney Page\n\n\nRead more \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/lifestyle/2026/07/12/duck-dog-formed-an-unlikely-friendship-now-theyre-inseparable/>\n <https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?text=A duck and a dog formed an unlikely \nfriendship. Now they’re \ninseparable.&url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.washingtonpost.com%2Flifestyle%2F2026%2F07%2F12%2Fduck-dog-formed-an-unlikely-friendship-now-theyre-inseparable%2F%3Futm_campaign%3Dwp_must_reads%26utm_medium%3Dsocial_share%26utm_source%3Dtwitter%26wpisrc%3Dnl_must-reads>\n \n<https://www.facebook.com/sharer/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.washingtonpost.com%2Flifestyle%2F2026%2F07%2F12%2Fduck-dog-formed-an-unlikely-friendship-now-theyre-inseparable%2F?utm_campaign=wp_must_reads&utm_medium=social_share&utm_source=facebook&wpisrc=nl_must-reads>\n <mailto:?subject=Check out this article from Must Reads newsletter&body=Hey! I \nthink you'd really like this article. Here is the link: \nhttps%3A%2F%2Fwww.washingtonpost.com%2Flifestyle%2F2026%2F07%2F12%2Fduck-dog-formed-an-unlikely-friendship-now-theyre-inseparable%2F%3Futm_campaign%3Dwp_must_reads%26utm_medium%3Dsocial_share%26utm_source%3Dnewsletter%26wpisrc%3Dnl_must-reads>\n\n\nA major Colorado River decision looms. Here’s how it will affect millions. \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/climate-environment/2026/07/15/colorado-river-decision-could-trigger-major-water-cuts-how-did-we-get-here/>\n \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/climate-environment/2026/07/15/colorado-river-decision-could-trigger-major-water-cuts-how-did-we-get-here/>\n (Emma Kumer/The Washington Post; Matthew Busch for The Post; iStock)\n\nWith the river in freefall, federal officials plan to intervene, possibly \nimposing large cuts to water usage in seven states. Effects would be felt \nbeyond the West. \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/climate-environment/2026/07/15/colorado-river-decision-could-trigger-major-water-cuts-how-did-we-get-here/>\n\nBy Sarah Kaplan\n\n\nRead more \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/climate-environment/2026/07/15/colorado-river-decision-could-trigger-major-water-cuts-how-did-we-get-here/>\n <https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?text=A major Colorado River decision looms. \nHere’s how it will affect \nmillions.&url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.washingtonpost.com%2Fclimate-environment%2F2026%2F07%2F15%2Fcolorado-river-decision-could-trigger-major-water-cuts-how-did-we-get-here%2F%3Futm_campaign%3Dwp_must_reads%26utm_medium%3Dsocial_share%26utm_source%3Dtwitter%26wpisrc%3Dnl_must-reads>\n \n<https://www.facebook.com/sharer/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.washingtonpost.com%2Fclimate-environment%2F2026%2F07%2F15%2Fcolorado-river-decision-could-trigger-major-water-cuts-how-did-we-get-here%2F?utm_campaign=wp_must_reads&utm_medium=social_share&utm_source=facebook&wpisrc=nl_must-reads>\n <mailto:?subject=Check out this article from Must Reads newsletter&body=Hey! I \nthink you'd really like this article. Here is the link: \nhttps%3A%2F%2Fwww.washingtonpost.com%2Fclimate-environment%2F2026%2F07%2F15%2Fcolorado-river-decision-could-trigger-major-water-cuts-how-did-we-get-here%2F%3Futm_campaign%3Dwp_must_reads%26utm_medium%3Dsocial_share%26utm_source%3Dnewsletter%26wpisrc%3Dnl_must-reads>\n\n\nThe death of the stick shift is almost here \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/transportation/2026/07/17/death-stick-shift-is-almost-here/>\n \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/transportation/2026/07/17/death-stick-shift-is-almost-here/>\n (iStock)\n\nDriving a manual vehicle is a lost art. Hopes of a revival have fallen flat. \nWith the rise of EVs and autonomous vehicles, only a few die-hards hold on. \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/transportation/2026/07/17/death-stick-shift-is-almost-here/>\n\nBy Todd Frankel\n\n\nRead more \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/transportation/2026/07/17/death-stick-shift-is-almost-here/>\n <https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?text=The death of the stick shift is almost \nhere&url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.washingtonpost.com%2Ftransportation%2F2026%2F07%2F17%2Fdeath-stick-shift-is-almost-here%2F%3Futm_campaign%3Dwp_must_reads%26utm_medium%3Dsocial_share%26utm_source%3Dtwitter%26wpisrc%3Dnl_must-reads>\n \n<https://www.facebook.com/sharer/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.washingtonpost.com%2Ftransportation%2F2026%2F07%2F17%2Fdeath-stick-shift-is-almost-here%2F?utm_campaign=wp_must_reads&utm_medium=social_share&utm_source=facebook&wpisrc=nl_must-reads>\n <mailto:?subject=Check out this article from Must Reads newsletter&body=Hey! I \nthink you'd really like this article. Here is the link: \nhttps%3A%2F%2Fwww.washingtonpost.com%2Ftransportation%2F2026%2F07%2F17%2Fdeath-stick-shift-is-almost-here%2F%3Futm_campaign%3Dwp_must_reads%26utm_medium%3Dsocial_share%26utm_source%3Dnewsletter%26wpisrc%3Dnl_must-reads>\n\n\nAn Ivy League professor suspected AI cheating, so he decided to fight back \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/education/2026/07/15/even-elite-colleges-are-scrambling-root-out-ai-cheating/>\n \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/education/2026/07/15/even-elite-colleges-are-scrambling-root-out-ai-cheating/>\n (Washington Post illustration; iStock)\n\nAI is advancing so quickly that colleges are struggling to find good ways to \ntest whether students are actually learning. \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/education/2026/07/15/even-elite-colleges-are-scrambling-root-out-ai-cheating/>\n\nBy Susan Svrluga\n\n\nRead more \n<https://www.washingtonpost.com/education/2026/07/15/even-elite-colleges-are-scrambling-root-out-ai-cheating/>\n <https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?text=An Ivy League professor suspected AI \ncheating, so he decided to fight \nback&url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.washingtonpost.com%2Feducation%2F2026%2F07%2F15%2Feven-elite-co